<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP Dependency-Check suppressions.

  A suppression is a claim that a reported CVE does not apply to this project, and it is only
  honest if it says why. Every entry MUST carry:

    * the CVE it suppresses,
    * a dated note explaining why this project is not affected (wrong component matched, the
      vulnerable code path is unreachable here, fixed in the version we actually use, …),
    * where possible, an <until> date, so the claim expires and has to be re-argued rather than
      quietly outliving the reason it was made.

  An empty file is the honest starting state: nothing has been waived. If the scan fails, the first
  response is to upgrade the dependency, not to add an entry here. Suppressing a finding to make a
  build green is exactly the move ENGINEERING_STANDARDS §4 exists to prevent — the same rule the
  ArchUnit and coverage gates get.
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">
</suppressions>
